.TH SPRITEEDIT 1
.SH NAME
spriteedit \- TempleOS-style vector sprite (element-list) editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B temple/spriteedit
[
.I file
[
.I name
]
]
.br
.B temple/spriteedit
.B -t
.I in
.I out
[
.I name
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Spriteedit
is a port of the TempleOS DolDoc sprite editor.
A sprite is not a grid of pixels but an ordered list of drawing
elements \- color and dither-color changes, pen thickness, planar
symmetry, points, lines, arrows, filled rectangles, circles, ellipses,
regular polygons, polylines, freehand poly-points, quadratic and cubic
B-splines (open and closed), flood fills, bitmaps, and text in the 8\(mu8
system font \- replayed in order through the drawing context.
The editor inserts, selects, and transforms those elements.
.PP
On disk each element is one text line, the same vocabulary that
.B scripts/tos_extract_sprites.py
writes into
.BR lib/temple/sprites/*.spr ,
so sprites move freely between the two:
.EX
color 4
thick 2
line 1 -15 7 -3
polygon 0 0 20 20 0.0 6
text -11 -12 'Hello'
.EE
A file may be such a bare element list (a
.B .spr
file), or the list may be embedded in any host text document between
.BI ".SPRITE " name
and
.BR .ENDSPRITE ;
saving replaces only the named block and preserves the surrounding
text, echoing DolDoc's sprites-inside-documents.
Legacy raster blocks
.RB ( ".SPRITE name w h r8g8b8a8" )
load as a single
.B bitmap
element.
A
.BI ".MESH " name
block opens the 3D mesh editor instead.
With no arguments the editor uses
.B /tmp/sprite.spr
and the name
.BR sprite .
.PP
The window shows the sprite replayed on a white page (origin at the
crosshair), with the element-list side bar on the left: every element as
a one-line summary, the red entry being the current insertion point.
Clicking an entry moves the insertion point; right-clicking an entry
selects or unselects that element.
New elements are inserted at the insertion point, and take their color,
thickness, and symmetry from the elements before it.
The 16-color TempleOS palette sits above the side bar; clicking a color
inserts a
.B color
element (the
.B T
cell inserts the transparent color).
The title bar tracks the current tool.
.PP
The
.B "Sprite Main Menu"
fills the canvas at start and whenever the right mouse button is
released; click an entry to arm a tool, then draw on the canvas with
rubber-band drags exactly as in TempleOS: lines, arrows, and rectangles
drag from anchor to release; circles drag out a radius; ellipses and
polygons drag out the radii, then turn to set the rotation and click;
polylines and B-splines are placed click by click and finished with the
right button; poly-points record a freehand stroke; text is typed first
and then placed with the mouse.
Flood fills are affected by whatever is underneath when they replay.
The screen-capture entries capture a dragged-out rectangle of the
editor's own canvas as a
.B bitmap
element (optionally making one palette color transparent), and
\(lqConvert to BitMap or Edit BitMap\(rq opens a zoomed pixel editor on
a bitmap element (left button paints the palette ink, right button
erases to transparent).
.PP
The
.B "Sprite Edit Menu"
holds the element operations: select/unselect all, select or unselect
elements by nearest control point or by dragged rectangles, shift
individual control points (4\(mu4 black/white handles; red when
selected), shift the selected elements, rotate or scale the selected
elements (rectangles promote to rotated rectangles, exactly as
TempleOS's transform does), set the origin, insert a shift sub-origin,
delete the selected elements, and insert another sprite file's elements
at the insertion point.
\(lqEdit as Text\(rq simply saves \- the on-disk form is already the
textual element code.
In the point modes,
.B p
and
.B r
switch between nearest-point and rectangle variants.
.SS Keys
.TP
.B Esc
Exit the sprite, keeping changes (in a sub-mode, back out one level).
.TP
.B q
Exit, saving.
.TP
.B A
Abort: restore the file as it was loaded, then exit.
.TP
.B c
Eye-dropper: insert a
.B color
element of the color under the mouse.
.TP
.B t
Insert the transparent color.
.TP
.B d
Delete the selected elements (in a menu).
.TP
.B s
Save.
.TP
.B l
Reload from disk.
.SS Mesh mode
A
.B .MESH
block opens the orthographic mesh editor: dragging moves the nearest
vertex in the current plane,
.B Tab
cycles XY/XZ/ZY,
.B v
places a vertex at the mouse,
.B Space
marks the selected vertex,
.B T
makes a triangle of the last three marks,
.B g
toggles a grid, and the palette row at the bottom sets the face color.
.SS Self test
.EX
temple/spriteedit -t in out [name]
.EE
loads
.I in
headlessly and saves it to
.IR out ;
element lists round-trip byte-identical.
.SH FILES
.TF lib/temple/spriteedit-demo.txt
.TP
.B lib/temple/sprites/*.spr
element lists extracted from the TempleOS distribution
.TP
.B lib/temple/spriteedit-demo.txt
a demo host document with embedded sprites and a mesh
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/temple/spriteedit.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR wm (1)
.SH BUGS
The sprite is edited over a plain white page rather than a live
document, control-point handles do not blink, and transforms move
bitmap elements without resampling them.
.B mesh
elements inside a sprite are inert placeholders; meshes are edited
through
.B .MESH
blocks.
